Not part of your question, but I noticed it while reading: `PATCH /orders/:id/cancel`, `PATCH /accounts/:id` and `DELETE /accounts/:id` never check that the target belongs to `req.user.accountId`. Any logged-in user can cancel another account's orders, or edit or delete another account.

No. `withClient` in `db.js` leaks connections, so a bigger pool would only take longer to fill up.

```js
const client = await pool.connect();
const result = await fn(client);
client.release();
```

If `fn` throws, the code never reaches `client.release()`, so that connection stays checked out for good. Each failed query permanently takes one of the 20 slots. `pool.log` fits that pattern: `idle=0` never recovers, and `waiting` climbs steadily from 7 to 28 over three minutes until requests start failing with `timeout exceeded when trying to connect`. That kind of steady climb looks like a leak, not a burst of traffic.

The log alone doesn't prove the leak is the only cause, but it is the one to rule out before you change `max`.

